e Code Remote session. Sends an interrupt control event — the target session's agent stops its current turn at the next checkpoint. Use this to pause a sibling session that's gone off-track before steering it with send_message.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__interrupt_session", "schema": {"description": "Interrupt a running Claude Code Remote session. Sends an interrupt control event — the target session's agent stops its current turn at the next checkpoint. Use this to pause a sibling session that's gone off-track before steering it with send_message.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"session_id": {"description": "The target session ID to interrupt.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["session_id"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__interrupt_session"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "List the folders on the user's own computers that THIS session can ask to use with request_computer_folder, and the folder this session is attached to, if any. The user normally chooses these folders on each computer, in the Claude desktop app: Settings, Claude Code, Remote Control. `folders` holds the ones this session can use: each has an environment_id, the computer's name, the folder's name, and whether the computer is online. An environment_id is an internal id that means nothing to the user: never show one to them or ask them to choose between ids; name the computer and the folder instead. `cannot_be_used` holds folders that are in the user's list but cannot be used from a cloud session, each with a reason and an explanation; they have no environment_id and cannot be requested. A folder can be used with or without Git, but only if it is not the home directory, is not inside a hidden directory directly under it (a name that starts with a dot) or ~/Library, and the Claude app or Claude Code on its computer is a version that can serve a folder from that kind of computer (on Windows, not every version can). If 