"}, "port": {"description": "The port the server really listens on, on this session's machine.", "maximum": 65535, "minimum": 1, "type": "integer"}}, "required": ["port"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__report_preview_port"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Connect THIS session to a folder the user's own computer serves through Remote Control, so this session's commands and file tools can run there. Calling this tool IS the request, and a person answers it every time (no permission mode and no stored rule does). So do NOT ask in chat first; once the task needs something that only exists on the user's computer, call list_computer_folders, then call this tool with the chosen folder's environment_id, computer_name and folder copied exactly — the request is refused if they do not match the folder's record. Only a folder under `folders` in that list can be requested; for one under `cannot_be_used`, give the user its explanation instead. The call waits for the computer. This can take a minute. The computer answers only while the Claude desktop app, or Claude Code's Remote Control, is running there. The result is final: `attached`, `refused` (the computer said no, and the result says why), or `did_not_answer` (no computer attached: it did not answer in time, or the user cancelled the request while it waited; nothing is left pending). A session is attached to at most one folder at a time, and it stays tied to the first computer it attaches to: to use a different computer, the user starts a new session. Asking again for the folder the session is attached to reconnects it: that attachment ends first, then the computer is asked afresh. While this session is attached to a folder (list_computer_folders shows it as `current`), do not call this tool for a different folder, and while an earlier request is still waiting, do not call it at all: the user would be shown the permission prompt, and the request is refused even if they allow it. If t